{"resourceIdentifier":"1900","resourceName":"Makame Savannah REDD","description":"Carbon Tanzania seeks to combine elements of conventional integrated conservation and development projects (ICDP) with a “payments for ecosystem services” (PES) approach when designing and implementing the Makame Savannah REDD project. REDD has at times been conceived narrowly as simply a system of conditional performance-based payments (PES)12. Projects developed by Carbon Tanzania are similarly premised on performance-based payments, but we have gone further in developing anapproach to project implementation that recognizes the part that can be played by tried and tested interventions. These include participatory land use mapping, boundary determination, development of land use plans, clarification of tenure, income generating activities, employment and community development.Following the principles of community-based natural resource management (CBNRM), the key underlying assumption of the Wildlife Management Area (WMA) concept is that providing local communities with economic benefits and involving them in the management13 of wildlife resources found in their areas will promote both the long-term health of wildlife, protect habitat and create rural economic development.Communities will thus have a vested interest in the conservation of natural resources because they benefit directly from sustainable management. WMAs were formally adopted as an approach for involving communities in wildlife management. WMAs can provide local residents with benefits through associated enterprises that sustainably utilize either wildlife or other natural resources in the WMA.The Makame Savannah REDD project will protect the Makame WMA from land grabbing and encroachment, and the conversion of land from forest to agriculture, thereby stopping deforestation and preventing GHG emissions generated through tree harvesting and clearing land for agriculture. In doing so the Masai communities living within the WMA will receive improved land tenure, grazing rights and significant income. Th","location":{"latitude":-4.665142,"longitude":36.654932},"inPublicCommentPeriod":false,"lastPublicCommentPeriod":{"startInclusive":"2025-10-03","endExclusive":"2025-11-03","endInclusive":"2025-11-02"},"attributes":[{"code":"PROJECT_ID","values":[{"type":"string","value":"1900"}]},{"code":"STATE_PROVINCE","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Kiteto District"}]}],"participationSummaries":[{"programCode":"VCS","attributes":[{"code":"PROPONENT_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Carbon Tanzania"},{"type":"string","value":"Kent, United Kingdom"},{"type":"string","value":"+447838538980"},{"type":"string","value":"jo@carbontanzania.com"}]},{"code":"PROJECT_STATUS","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Registered"},{"type":"assetSearchLink","value":null,"targetId":"1900"},{"type":"bufferSearchLink","value":null,"targetId":"1900"}]},{"code":"EST_ANNUAL_EMISSION_REDCT","values":[{"type":"string","value":"136411"}]},{"code":"TOTAL_BUFFER_POOL_CREDITS","values":[{"type":"integer","value":450255}]},{"code":"PRIMARY_PROJECT_CATEGORY_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Agriculture Forestry and Other Land Use"}]},{"code":"PROJECT_SUBCATERGORY_NAMES","values":[{"type":"string","value":"REDD"}]},{"code":"PROTOCOL_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"VM0007"}]},{"code":"PROJECT_ACREAGE","values":[{"type":"string","value":"104065 Hectares"}]},{"code":"VALIDATOR_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"EPIC Sustainability Services Private Limited"}]},{"code":"PROJECT_REGISTRATION_DATE","values":[{"type":"string","value":"02/10/2020"}]},{"code":"CREDIT_PERIOD_INFO","values":[{"type":"string","value":"1st, 27/04/2016 - 26/04/2046"}]}]},{"programCode":"CCB","attributes":[{"code":"PROPONENT_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Carbon Tanzania"},{"type":"string","value":"Kent, United Kingdom"},{"type":"string","value":"+447838538980"},{"type":"string","value":"jo@carbontanzania.com"}]},{"co